    </node>
    <node id="Global Sensemaking Question Generation">
      <data key="d2">Global Sensemaking Question Generation</data>
      <data key="d3">Method</data>
      <data key="d4">A process using an LLM to generate corpus-specific questions designed to assess high-level understanding without retrieving specific low-level facts.</data>
      <data key="d5">To evaluate the effectiveness of RAG systems for global sensemaking tasks, we use an LLM to generate a set of corpus-specific questions designed to asses high-level understanding of a given corpus</data>
      <data key="d6">[6]</data>
      <data key="d7">["To evaluate the effectiveness of RAG systems for global sensemaking tasks, we use an LLM to generate a set of corpus-specific questions designed to asses high-level understanding of a given corpus"]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Graph Communities">
      <data key="d2">Graph Communities</data>
      <data key="d3">Concept</data>
      <data key="d4">A hierarchy providing community partitions that cover graph nodes in a mutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive way.</data>
      <data key="d5">Each level of this hierarchy provides a community partition that covers the nodes of the graph in a mutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive way</data>
      <data key="d6">[6]</data>
      <data key="d7">["Each level of this hierarchy provides a community partition that covers the nodes of the graph in a mutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive way"]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Graph indexing">
      <data key="d2">Graph indexing</data>
      <data key="d3">Method</data>
      <data key="d4">A method using a 600 token window that took 281 minutes for the Podcast dataset.</data>
      <data key="d5">Graph indexing with a 600 token window (explained in Section A.2) took 281 minutes for the Podcast dataset</data>
      <data key="d6">[9]</data>
      <data key="d7">["Graph indexing with a 600 token window (explained in Section A.2) took 281 minutes for the Podcast dataset"]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GraphRAG">
      <data key="d2">GraphRAG</data>
      <data key="d3">Method</data>
      <data key="d4">A graph-based approach to question answering over private text corpora that scales with the generality of user questions and the quantity of source text.</data>
      <data key="d5">we propose GraphRAG, a graph-based approach to question answering over private text corpora that scales with both the generality of user questions and the quantity of source text. | In this paper, we present GraphRAG – a graph-based RAG approach that enables sensemaking over the entirety of a large text corpus. | GraphRAG builds upon prior work on advanced RAG strategies. | Graph RAG pipeline using an LLM-derived graph index of source document text. | Furthermore, GraphRAG is generally resilient to duplicate entities since duplicates are typically clustered together for summarization in subsequent steps. | GraphRAG generates community summaries by adding various element summaries (for nodes, edges, and related claims) to a community summary template | "We compared six conditions including GraphRAG at four different graph community levels (C0, C1, C2, C3)..." | All Graph RAG conditions outperformed na¨ıve RAG on comprehensiveness and diversity. | "We have presented GraphRAG, a RAG approach that combines knowledge graph generation and query-focused summarization (QFS) to support human sensemaking over entire text corpora."</data>
      <data key="d6">[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12]</data>
      <data key="d7">["we propose GraphRAG, a graph-based approach to question answering over private text corpora that scales with both the generality of user questions and the quantity of source text.", "In this paper, we present GraphRAG – a graph-based RAG approach that enables sensemaking over the entirety of a large text corpus.", "GraphRAG builds upon prior work on advanced RAG strategies.", "Graph RAG pipeline using an LLM-derived graph index of source document text.", "Furthermore, GraphRAG is generally resilient to duplicate entities since duplicates are typically clustered together for summarization in subsequent steps.", "GraphRAG generates community summaries by adding various element summaries (for nodes, edges, and related claims) to a community summary template", "\"We compared six conditions including GraphRAG at four different graph community levels (C0, C1, C2, C3)...\"", "All Graph RAG conditions outperformed na¨ıve RAG on comprehensiveness and diversity.", "\"We have presented GraphRAG, a RAG approach that combines knowledge graph generation and query-focused summarization (QFS) to support human sensemaking over entire text corpora.\""]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Higher-level communities">
      <data key="d2">Higher-level communities</data>
      <data key="d3">Concept</data>
      <data key="d4">Communities above the leaf level where sub-community summaries are substituted for element summaries if token limits are reached.</data>
      <data key="d5">Higher-level communities. If all element summaries fit within the token limit of the context window, proceed as for leaf-level communities and summarize all element summaries within the community</data>
      <data key="d6">[6]</data>
      <data key="d7">["Higher-level communities. If all element summaries fit within the token limit of the context window, proceed as for leaf-level communities and summarize all element summaries within the community"]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Holm-Bonferroni correction">
      <data key="d2">Holm-Bonferroni correction</data>
      <data key="d3">Method</data>
      <data key="d4">A correction applied to account for multiple pairwise comparisons.</data>
      <data key="d5">with Holm-Bonferroni correction applied to account for multiple pairwise comparisons.</data>
      <data key="d6">[26]</data>
      <data key="d7">["with Holm-Bonferroni correction applied to account for multiple pairwise comparisons."]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HotPotQA">
      <data key="d2">HotPotQA</data>
      <data key="d3">Dataset</data>
      <data key="d4">A benchmark dataset for open-domain question answering oriented towards vector RAG performance.</data>
      <data key="d5">Many benchmark datasets for open-domain question answering exist, including HotPotQA</data>
      <data key="d6">[3]</data>
      <data key="d7">["Many benchmark datasets for open-domain question answering exist, including HotPotQA"]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knowledge Graph">
      <data key="d2">Knowledge Graph</data>
      <data key="d3">Model</data>
      <data key="d4">A graph index derived from source documents in the first stage of GraphRAG's approach.</data>
      <data key="d5">first, to derive an entity knowledge graph from the source documents | GraphRAG first uses an LLM to construct a knowledge graph, where nodes correspond to key entities in the corpus and edges represent relationships between those entities. | In the final step of the knowledge graph extraction process, these instances of entities and relationships become individual nodes and edges in the graph.</data>
      <data key="d6">[1, 2, 5]</data>
      <data key="d7">["first, to derive an entity knowledge graph from the source documents", "GraphRAG first uses an LLM to construct a knowledge graph, where nodes correspond to key entities in the corpus and edges represent relationships between those entities.", "In the final step of the knowledge graph extraction process, these instances of entities and relationships become individual nodes and edges in the graph."]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LLM">
      <data key="d2">LLM</data>
      <data key="d3">Model</data>
      <data key="d4">A large language model used to build a graph index and generate responses in the GraphRAG approach.</data>
      <data key="d5">Our approach uses an LLM to build a graph index in two stages | LLM prompts tailored to the domain of the dataset. | The LLM is prompted such that it extracts the following: The entity NeoChip... | The LLM is also asked to generate a score between 0-100 indicating how helpful the generated answer is in answering the target question | prompt the LLM to | "In our evaluations, the LLM is provided with the question, the generated answers from two competing systems, and prompted to compare the two answers according to the criterion before giving a final judgment..."</data>
      <data key="d6">[1, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]</data>
      <data key="d7">["Our approach uses an LLM to build a graph index in two stages", "LLM prompts tailored to the domain of the dataset.", "The LLM is prompted such that it extracts the following: The entity NeoChip...", "The LLM is also asked to generate a score between 0-100 indicating how helpful the generated answer is in answering the target question", "prompt the LLM to", "\"In our evaluations, the LLM is provided with the question, the generated answers from two competing systems, and prompted to compare the two answers according to the criterion before giving a final judgment...\""]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LLM-as-a-judge">
      <data key="d2">LLM-as-a-judge</data>
      <data key="d3">Method</data>
      <data key="d4">A technique using one LLM to generate questions and a second LLM to judge answers of different RAG systems.</data>
      <data key="d5">we developed a novel application of the LLM-as-a-judge technique (Zheng et al., 2024) suitable for questions targeting broad issues and themes where there is no ground-truth answer.</data>
      <data key="d6">[2]</data>
      <data key="d7">["we developed a novel application of the LLM-as-a-judge technique (Zheng et al., 2024) suitable for questions targeting broad issues and themes where there is no ground-truth answer."]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LLM-based persona generation">
      <data key="d2">LLM-based persona generation</data>
      <data key="d3">Method</data>
      <data key="d4">A prior work approach where an LLM generates diverse and authentic sets of personas to guide query generation.</data>
      <data key="d5">Our approach builds on prior work in LLM-based persona generation, where the LLM is used to generate diverse and authentic sets of personas</data>
      <data key="d6">[3]</data>
      <data key="d7">["Our approach builds on prior work in LLM-based persona generation, where the LLM is used to generate diverse and authentic sets of personas"]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LangChain">
      <data key="d2">LangChain</data>
      <data key="d3">Framework</data>
      <data key="d4">Open-source framework that provides an extension integrating the GraphRAG approach.</data>
      <data key="d5">versions of the GraphRAG approach are also available as extensions to multiple open-source libraries, including LangChain (LangChain, 2024)</data>
      <data key="d6">[2]</data>
      <data key="d7">["versions of the GraphRAG approach are also available as extensions to multiple open-source libraries, including LangChain (LangChain, 2024)"]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leaf-level communities">
      <data key="d2">Leaf-level communities</data>
      <data key="d3">Concept</data>
      <data key="d4">The lowest level of the community hierarchy where element summaries are prioritized and added to the context window.</data>
      <data key="d5">Leaf-level communities. The element summaries of a leaf-level community are prioritized and then iteratively added to the LLM context window until the token limit is reached</data>
      <data key="d6">[6]</data>
      <data key="d7">["Leaf-level communities. The element summaries of a leaf-level community are prioritized and then iteratively added to the LLM context window until the token limit is reached"]</data>
