    <node id="query-focused summarization">
      <data key="d2">query-focused summarization</data>
      <data key="d3">Concept</data>
      <data key="d4">A task type where RAG fails on global questions directed at an entire text corpus, such as identifying main themes in a dataset.</data>
      <data key="d5">since this is inherently a query-focused summarization (QFS) task, rather than an explicit retrieval task.</data>
      <data key="d6">[1]</data>
      <data key="d7">["since this is inherently a query-focused summarization (QFS) task, rather than an explicit retrieval task."]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="self-memory">
      <data key="d2">self-memory</data>
      <data key="d3">Concept</data>
      <data key="d4">Summaries over large sections of the data source used to answer queries.</data>
      <data key="d5">GraphRAG leverages summaries over large sections of the data source as a form of "self-memory"</data>
      <data key="d6">[3]</data>
      <data key="d7">["GraphRAG leverages summaries over large sections of the data source as a form of \"self-memory\""]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sensemaking">
      <data key="d2">sensemaking</data>
      <data key="d3">Concept</data>
      <data key="d4">Tasks requiring reasoning over connections among people, places, and events to anticipate trajectories.</data>
      <data key="d5">Sensemaking tasks require reasoning over "connections (which can be among people, places, and events) in order to anticipate their trajectories and act effectively" (Klein et al., 2006).</data>
      <data key="d6">[2]</data>
      <data key="d7">["Sensemaking tasks require reasoning over \"connections (which can be among people, places, and events) in order to anticipate their trajectories and act effectively\" (Klein et al., 2006)."]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sensemaking activity">
      <data key="d2">sensemaking activity</data>
      <data key="d3">Concept</data>
      <data key="d4">The type of iterative question answering characterized by the use of root-level GraphRAG.</data>
      <data key="d5">root-level GraphRAG offers a highly efficient method for the iterative question answering that characterizes sensemaking activity</data>
      <data key="d6">[10]</data>
      <data key="d7">["root-level GraphRAG offers a highly efficient method for the iterative question answering that characterizes sensemaking activity"]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="vector RAG">
      <data key="d2">vector RAG</data>
      <data key="d3">Method</data>
      <data key="d4">A conventional RAG approach using text embeddings to retrieve semantically similar records for query responses.</data>
      <data key="d5">This conventional approach, which we collectively call vector RAG, works well for queries that can be answered with information localized within a small set of records. | retaining advantages in comprehensiveness (72% win rate) and diversity (62% win rate) over vector RAG. | "Compared to vector RAG, however, GraphRAG shows promise as a way to mitigate these downstream risks for questions of a global nature..."</data>
      <data key="d6">[2, 10, 12]</data>
      <data key="d7">["This conventional approach, which we collectively call vector RAG, works well for queries that can be answered with information localized within a small set of records.", "retaining advantages in comprehensiveness (72% win rate) and diversity (62% win rate) over vector RAG.", "\"Compared to vector RAG, however, GraphRAG shows promise as a way to mitigate these downstream risks for questions of a global nature...\""]</data>
      <data key="d8">semantically_reviewed</data>
    </node>
